        "contact": {
          "name": "GitHub Repository",
          "url": "https://github.com/prakhardubey2002/shame-as-a-service"
        },
        "description": "A simple NestJS API that delivers country-specific shame messages. Perfect for adding a bit of humor to your applications or motivating your team with culturally-aware shame quotes. Because logs warn you, errors stop you, but shame changes you.",
        "license": {
          "name": "MIT",
          "url": "https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT"
        },
        "servers": [
          {
            "description": "Production server",
            "url": "https://shame-as-a-service.vercel.app"
          },
          {
            "description": "Local development server",
            "url": "http://localhost:3000"
          }
        ],
        "title": "Shame as a Service",
        "version": "1.0.0",
        "tagline": "Get a country-specific shame message based on your IP geolocation",
        "homepage": "https://shame-as-a-service.vercel.app",
        "docs_url": "https://freepublicapis.com/shame-as-a-service",
        "license_short": "MIT",
        "license_md": "- Released under the MIT licence\n- Free for personal and commercial use with attribution to the project\n- Provided as-is with no warranty",
        "about_md": "Shame as a Service is a small NestJS-powered API that returns a humorous, country-themed shame message based on the caller's IP geolocation. The service is hosted on Vercel at [shame-as-a-service.vercel.app](https://shame-as-a-service.vercel.app).\n\nWhat you get from the API:\n\n- A JSON response with `message` (the shame quote), `country` (the detected country, or `unknown`), and `ip` (the resolved caller IP)\n- Country detection driven by the requesting IP address\n- A single GET endpoint at the service root\n\nOperational notes: the service is publicly reachable without authentication. CORS is reported as disabled, so browser-based callers may need a proxy. Because country selection depends on the caller IP, requests from servers, CDNs, or VPN exit nodes can return `unknown` or a country that does not match the end user.",
        "entity_desc": {
          "get_shame_message": "Returns a single shame message tailored to the caller's detected country, with the message, country code, and resolved IP in the JSON body."
        },
